[# Section 13 — Comparison to alternatives. Back-matter; appears
   after §12 FAQ. Authored 2026-05-22.

   The goal is to help an evaluator decide whether to adopt CX. Be
   honest where CX loses — ecosystem maturity, library breadth,
   tooling familiarity — and concrete where it wins. Cross-links use
   [[anchor]] form. File extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=comparison n=13 title='Comparison to alternatives'

  [intro
    """CX is one of many serialization / config / wire formats. This
       section is the honest side-by-side: what CX brings, where the
       incumbents are better, and which workloads should pick which
       tool. Each comparison covers feature parity, performance,
       ecosystem, learning curve, schema, identity / hash, and
       round-trip lossiness, with a closing "when to pick X"
       sentence."""]

  [child id=when-to-use n=13.1 title='When to use CX'
    [intro
      """The decision tree. Top-to-bottom, first match wins:"""]

    [list
      [item """**You need one syntax for data and code** (config that
              is also a program, templates that are also data, a
              schema that is also a value). CX is the only format
              in this comparison that is homoiconic; pick CX."""]
      [item """**You need content-addressable identity** across
              languages and platforms (cache keys, dedup, signed
              documents, distributed ledgers). The CX SHA-256 of
              canonical bytes is the most boring possible
              implementation; pick CX."""]
      [item """**You round-trip between JSON / YAML / TOML / XML /
              Markdown** and lose information each time. CX is the
              superset that preserves all of them losslessly; pick
              CX."""]
      [item """**Your workload is bulk analytical scan on tabular
              data** at terabyte scale, exchanged with the broader
              ecosystem (DuckDB / Spark / pandas). Use **Parquet**
              for storage, **Arrow** for in-memory, optionally
              with CX as the metadata / config layer."""]
      [item """**Your workload is wire-format throughput between
              microservices** with a fixed schema, and you control
              both ends. Use **Protocol Buffers** or **MessagePack**
              — they will be smaller and faster on the wire than
              any text format."""]
      [item """**You need a single-file human-edited config** with
              no programmability and no cross-system identity
              requirements. **TOML** (Cargo, pyproject) or **YAML**
              (Kubernetes, CI) are everywhere; the ecosystem
              advantage is real."""]
      [item """**You need a typed, programmable config language**
              with a strong type system. **Dhall** or **Pkl** are
              good fits if the type-system rigor matters more than
              the homoiconic data-and-code unification."""]
      [item """**Otherwise** (general data / config / interchange
              with mixed workloads, mid-size documents, multiple
              consumers in different languages): CX is the
              all-purpose default."""]]]

  [child id=cx-vs-json n=13.2 title='CX vs JSON'
    [intro
      """JSON is the universal interchange baseline. CX is a
         superset projection — anything that round-trips through
         JSON also round-trips through CX. The interesting
         comparisons are where CX has features JSON does not, and
         where JSON has ecosystem JSON does not."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, json, cx)
      [row dimension='Schema' json='JSON Schema (separate spec)'
           cx='.cxs — same syntax as data (see [[schema]])']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' json='No canonical form; hash drifts on key reorder'
           cx='SHA-256 of canonical bytes; stable across platforms (see [[hash]])']
      [row dimension='Comments' json='None (in the standard); JSON5 / JSONC are extensions'
           cx='[# … #] preserved by cx fmt, stripped by cx canon']
      [row dimension='Trailing commas' json='Forbidden' cx='Not applicable — bracket form, no commas in attribute list']
      [row dimension='Number precision' json='Underspecified; JS doubles by default'
           cx='Sized type tags (:i64, :u32, :f32) (see [[sized-types]])']
      [row dimension='Multi-line strings' json='Escaped only' cx='Triple-quoted, whitespace-preserving (see [[strings]])']
      [row dimension='Date / datetime' json='String only' cx='First-class :date / :datetime scalar (see [[dates]])']
      [row dimension='Bytes' json='Base64 in a string' cx='First-class :bytes scalar (see [[bytes]])']
      [row dimension='Tooling ecosystem' json='Vast — every language, every editor, every CDN'
           cx='Tier-1 bindings V/Python/Go/Rust; LSP + tree-sitter + VS Code; growing']
      [row dimension='Wire size' json='Baseline' cx='Comparable text form; data-bin / CXCol smaller']
      [row dimension='Parse speed' json='Highly optimized parsers everywhere'
           cx='Reference parser ~600 MB/s on the gate-15 workload']
      [row dimension='Learning curve' json='Trivial' cx='Bracket-and-sigil form; ~one hour for the data subset']]

    [body
      """**When to pick JSON instead of CX.** Your consumers are
         browser JavaScript, a CDN-served config endpoint, or any
         interface where you cannot install a CX parser. JSON
         remains the right wire-format choice for ubiquity
         alone."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX instead of JSON.** You want identity,
         schema, comments, mixed content, dates, or bytes without
         layering JSON Schema + JSON5 + base64 + custom date
         string conventions on top of JSON. CX gives you the
         whole stack in one syntax."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-yaml n=13.3 title='CX vs YAML'
    [intro
      """YAML optimises for human readability of multi-line
         strings and config-as-code. The cost is the famously
         large surface area (indentation sensitivity, tag system,
         anchor / alias / merge keys, the Norway problem)."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, yaml, cx)
      [row dimension='Indentation' yaml='Semantic (whitespace-sensitive)'
           cx='Insignificant — bracket-structured']
      [row dimension='Anchors and aliases' yaml='&anchor / *alias / << merge keys'
           cx='&anchor / *merge sigils — same idea, no separate merge keyword (see [[anchor]] / [[merge]])']
      [row dimension='Multiline strings' yaml='| (literal) and > (folded)'
           cx='Triple-quoted (always literal)']
      [row dimension='Norway problem' yaml='no parses as false in YAML 1.1' cx='Only true/false are bools; no /yes is a string']
      [row dimension='Schema' yaml='JSON Schema (separate)' cx='.cxs (same syntax)']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' yaml='No canonical form'
           cx='SHA-256 of canonical bytes (see [[hash]])']
      [row dimension='Comments' yaml='# line comments only'
           cx='[# … #] nest, span lines, preserved by cx fmt']
      [row dimension='Tooling' yaml='Kubernetes, GitLab CI, Ansible'
           cx='Growing — LSP + tree-sitter + VS Code; analytical pipelines']
      [row dimension='Round-trip' yaml='Lossy in many YAML 1.1 → 1.2 cases'
           cx='Lossless by spec (see [[roundtrip]])']]

    [body
      """**When to pick YAML.** Your config consumers are
         Kubernetes manifests, Ansible playbooks, GitHub Actions
         workflows, or any tool whose schema is in YAML. The
         ecosystem advantage is decisive."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX.** Anywhere you would have wanted YAML
         minus the indentation footguns, the Norway problem, and
         the lossy round-trip. The triple-quoted multiline string
         is as readable as YAML literal blocks without the
         indentation rules."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-toml n=13.4 title='CX vs TOML'
    [intro
      """TOML's pitch is "obviously correct config for
         configuration files." It excels at flat key-value
         configs (Cargo.toml, pyproject.toml) and struggles with
         deep nesting."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, toml, cx)
      [row dimension='Nesting' toml='Dotted keys / [[arrays of tables]] — awkward past 2-3 levels'
           cx='Native — every element nests']
      [row dimension='Schema' toml='No standard schema language' cx='.cxs (see [[schema]])']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' toml='No canonical form'
           cx='SHA-256 of canonical bytes']
      [row dimension='Tooling' toml='Cargo, pip, Poetry — narrow but deep'
           cx='LSP + tree-sitter; broader workloads than configs alone']
      [row dimension='Round-trip' toml='Lossy — comment placement and key order'
           cx='Lossless']
      [row dimension='Mixed content' toml='Not supported' cx='Native (prose form, see [[prose]])']
      [row dimension='Date / datetime' toml='Native :datetime' cx='Native :date / :datetime / :time']]

    [body
      """**When to pick TOML.** Your config is flat — language
         toolchain manifests, Cargo dependencies, app
         configuration with two levels of nesting. TOML reads
         beautifully at that scale."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX.** Your config is genuinely
         hierarchical, you need cross-config identity, or the
         same documents will also be processed as data (e.g.
         shipped as configuration *and* hashed for a release
         manifest). CX scales up where TOML hits its ceiling."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-xml n=13.5 title='CX vs XML'
    [intro
      """XML is CX's closest structural cousin — both are
         tree-shaped, both support attributes and mixed content,
         both have namespaces. The differences are syntactic
         density and the surrounding ecosystem (XSLT, XPath,
         XSD)."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, xml, cx)
      [row dimension='Syntax verbosity' xml='Open + close tag pair' cx='Single bracket pair']
      [row dimension='Attribute syntax' xml='name="value" inside open tag'
           cx='name=value inside bracket (see [[attributes]])']
      [row dimension='Mixed content' xml='Native' cx='Native (see [[prose]])']
      [row dimension='Namespaces' xml='xmlns:prefix=URI' cx='xmlns:prefix=URI (XML-aligned, see [[namespaces]])']
      [row dimension='Selector' xml='XPath 3.1' cx='CXPath — XPath 3.1 aligned (see [[cxpath]])']
      [row dimension='Schema' xml='XSD (large, complex) or RELAX NG' cx='.cxs (CX syntax, small)']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' xml='C14N (XML Canonicalization)'
           cx='cx canon + SHA-256']
      [row dimension='Templating' xml='XSLT (separate language)'
           cx='[?for] / [?modify] — same language as data']
      [row dimension='Tooling' xml='Vast in enterprise / publishing; less in modern web'
           cx='Growing; same ecosystem reach is the long-term goal']
      [row dimension='Learning curve' xml='Familiar to a generation of developers'
           cx='Lower if XML is new; comparable if XML is known']]

    [body
      """**When to pick XML.** You are working in publishing /
         document workflows (DITA, DocBook, SVG), or your
         consumers are XML-native (SOAP, XHTML, RDF/XML). The
         tooling depth is unmatched in those niches."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX.** You want the XML data model
         (attributes, mixed content, namespaces, XPath) without
         the syntactic verbosity, without XSD, and with code-as-
         data unification. CX is what XML would look like if
         designed today."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-protobuf n=13.6 title='CX vs Protocol Buffers'
    [intro
      """Protobuf is the schema-first binary wire format for
         service-to-service traffic. It is the right tool when
         you control both ends, the schema rarely changes, and
         every byte on the wire matters."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, protobuf, cx)
      [row dimension='Wire format' protobuf='Binary — varint + length-prefix'
           cx='Text (CX), or data-bin / CXCol (binary)']
      [row dimension='Schema requirement' protobuf='Required — wire is unparseable without .proto'
           cx='Optional — value is self-describing']
      [row dimension='Wire size' protobuf='Typically smallest of any format here'
           cx='Comparable text size; data-bin closer to protobuf']
      [row dimension='Throughput' protobuf='Very high — compiled (de)serializers'
           cx='High but text-shaped; data-bin closes the gap']
      [row dimension='Schema evolution' protobuf='Tag-numbered fields — strong forward/backward compat'
           cx='Schema modes (open/strict/closed); attribute order is data-only']
      [row dimension='Tooling' protobuf='protoc + every major language'
           cx='Tier-1 V/Python/Go/Rust; growing']
      [row dimension='Human readability' protobuf='None — binary opaque'
           cx='Text-first; binary is an optimization']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' protobuf='Wire bytes — stable iff schema is fixed'
           cx='Canonical bytes — stable regardless of schema']]

    [body
      """**When to pick Protobuf.** You have a controlled RPC
         system, the schema is well-understood, and the bandwidth
         / CPU savings are real (mobile, embedded, hot-path
         services). Honest concession: Protobuf wins on wire
         throughput in the steady state."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX.** Your data is also config, also
         logs, also human-edited at the boundary, also stored on
         disk for later inspection. Protobuf loses to text
         formats outside the wire-only path."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-msgpack n=13.7 title='CX vs MessagePack'
    [intro
      """MessagePack is "binary JSON" — schemaless, more compact
         than text JSON, slightly slower than protobuf because
         it carries kind tags."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, msgpack, cx)
      [row dimension='Wire format' msgpack='Binary, schemaless' cx='Text (CX) or binary (data-bin)']
      [row dimension='Schema' msgpack='None' cx='.cxs (optional)']
      [row dimension='Wire size' msgpack='Smaller than JSON; comparable to data-bin'
           cx='data-bin similar; CXCol smaller for tabular']
      [row dimension='Type richness' msgpack='Scalars + map + array; ext-type extension'
           cx='Eight scalar kinds, elements with attributes, mixed content, namespaces']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' msgpack='Wire bytes — stable iff serializer is deterministic'
           cx='Canonical bytes — stable by construction']
      [row dimension='Tooling' msgpack='Wide language coverage'
           cx='Tier-1 V/Python/Go/Rust']]

    [body
      """**When to pick MessagePack.** You want JSON semantics
         on the wire without JSON wire size, and your data is
         flat / tabular / homogeneous. MessagePack is excellent
         at being "JSON minus the text."."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX.** Your data has shape — attributes,
         mixed content, namespaces, hierarchical structure that
         MessagePack would force into nested maps. data-bin
         covers the wire-size argument without giving up the
         shape."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-pkl n=13.8 title='CX vs Apache Pkl'
    [intro
      """Pkl is Apple's configuration language — declarative,
         strongly typed, programmable. The closest "config that
         is also code" peer to CX, with a different design
         centre."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, pkl, cx)
      [row dimension='Surface' pkl='Custom syntax — class-oriented' cx='Bracket form — homoiconic']
      [row dimension='Typing' pkl='Strong, static, gradual'
           cx='Schema-validated (.cxs); type tags on scalars']
      [row dimension='Computation' pkl='Lambdas, comprehensions, methods'
           cx='Directives — [?for], [?match], [?modify], [?let]']
      [row dimension='Output formats' pkl='JSON / YAML / Plist / Properties + custom renderers'
           cx='CX / JSON / YAML / TOML / XML / Markdown / CSV / JSONL']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' pkl='Not built-in' cx='SHA-256 of canonical bytes']
      [row dimension='Module system' pkl='Imports, packages, semantic versioning'
           cx='[?cx include] — capability-gated path resolution']
      [row dimension='Tooling' pkl='LSP, IntelliJ plugin, CLI — solid'
           cx='LSP + tree-sitter + VS Code — comparable']
      [row dimension='Ecosystem' pkl='Apple-backed; growing community'
           cx='Open-source; growing community']]

    [body
      """**When to pick Pkl.** You want a strongly typed config
         language with class-oriented composition and your
         consumers are JSON / YAML output. Pkl's type system is
         richer than the CX schema language."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX.** The same documents are also data
         to be hashed, queried, transformed, or shipped as
         payloads. Pkl is config-first; CX is data-and-code by
         design."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-dhall n=13.9 title='CX vs Dhall'
    [intro
      """Dhall is the typed-config purist's pick — total,
         non-Turing-complete, with a sound type system and
         normalised import semantics."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, dhall, cx)
      [row dimension='Type system' dhall='Total, sound, dependent-ish (System Fω)'
           cx='Schema validation; type tags on scalars']
      [row dimension='Computation' dhall='λ-calculus — total, no recursion without bound'
           cx='Directives — [?for] / [?match] / [?modify]; bounded by resilience directives']
      [row dimension='Surface' dhall='Haskell-flavoured ML syntax' cx='Bracket form']
      [row dimension='Output formats' dhall='JSON / YAML / Text' cx='CX / JSON / YAML / TOML / XML / Markdown / CSV / JSONL']
      [row dimension='Imports' dhall='Content-addressed by hash — same idea as CX'
           cx='[?cx include] with capability gating']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' dhall='Standard form hash for imports'
           cx='Canonical bytes + SHA-256 for any value']
      [row dimension='Learning curve' dhall='Steep — ML / Haskell familiarity helps'
           cx='Moderate — bracket form is unfamiliar but small']
      [row dimension='Tooling' dhall='LSP, type-aware; narrow' cx='LSP + tree-sitter + VS Code']]

    [body
      """**When to pick Dhall.** You want totality, soundness,
         and a small surface area as design goals; you are
         willing to pay for the steeper learning curve. Dhall is
         the right tool for safety-critical config."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CX.** You want the content-addressed
         identity, the schema-validated config, and the
         programmable updates of Dhall — plus the ability to
         carry rich data (mixed content, columnar tables,
         dates, bytes) in the same syntax. Dhall is config; CX
         is config + data + code."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-parquet n=13.10 title='CX vs Parquet'
    [intro
      """Parquet is the columnar storage standard for analytical
         workloads — terabyte-scale scans, predicate pushdown,
         compression. CX is not Parquet's competitor; CXCol is,
         and Parquet is CX's export target."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, parquet, cx-cxcol)
      [row dimension='Layout' parquet='Column-oriented, on-disk'
           cx-cxcol='Column-oriented (CXCol), in-memory; on-disk possible']
      [row dimension='Compression' parquet='Snappy / Gzip / Zstd / LZ4 page-level'
           cx-cxcol='Dictionary encoding on; compression optional']
      [row dimension='Schema' parquet='Embedded — required for read'
           cx-cxcol='.cxs schema or embedded type tags']
      [row dimension='Ecosystem' parquet='Spark, DuckDB, Polars, pandas, Athena, BigQuery'
           cx-cxcol='Native CX pipelines; Arrow bridge for everything else']
      [row dimension='Bridge' parquet='—'
           cx-cxcol='CX ↔ Parquet bridge via Arrow (see [[parquet-bridge]])']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' parquet='File hash — sensitive to write order'
           cx-cxcol='Canonical bytes of the logical value']
      [row dimension='Streaming' parquet='Row-group oriented'
           cx-cxcol='Native streaming evaluator (see [[streaming]])']
      [row dimension='Write speed' parquet='~150 MB/s on the reference benchmark'
           cx-cxcol='Faster — no compression overhead by default']]

    [body
      """**When to pick Parquet.** Your data is large, tabular,
         queried by external tools (Spark / Athena / BigQuery /
         DuckDB), and stored on object storage. Parquet is the
         lingua franca."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CXCol.** Your data is tabular but the
         producers and consumers are CX-native (pipelines, in-
         memory analytical jobs, services that ship columnar
         payloads). When you need to interchange with the
         broader ecosystem, export to Parquet."""]]

  [child id=cx-vs-arrow n=13.11 title='CX vs Arrow'
    [intro
      """Arrow is the in-memory columnar standard. Like
         MessagePack, it is not a CX competitor — it is a layer
         CX integrates with."""]

    [table columns=(dimension, arrow, cx-cxcol)
      [row dimension='Layout' arrow='Column-oriented, in-memory'
           cx-cxcol='Column-oriented, in-memory (compatible)']
      [row dimension='Wire format' arrow='Flight (gRPC streaming) / IPC'
           cx-cxcol='data-bin chunked tables']
      [row dimension='Interop' arrow='pandas, Polars, DuckDB, Datafusion — zero-copy'
           cx-cxcol='CX ↔ Arrow bridge (zero-copy, see [[arrow-bridge]])']
      [row dimension='Schema' arrow='Embedded' cx-cxcol='.cxs or embedded']
      [row dimension='Identity / hash' arrow='None standard' cx-cxcol='Canonical bytes + SHA-256']
      [row dimension='Use case' arrow='Cross-process / cross-language analytics'
           cx-cxcol='Same plus document semantics']]

    [body
      """**When to pick Arrow.** You are doing pure analytics —
         no document semantics, no identity requirements, no
         schema as a value. Arrow is the right cross-engine
         in-memory layout."""]

    [body
      """**When to pick CXCol over Arrow.** You want Arrow's
         column-orientation plus CX's document semantics (mixed
         content rows, hierarchical headers, namespaced columns,
         hash-stable identity). Use the bridge — they share
         memory zero-copy."""]]

  [child id=cmp-summary n=13.12 title='Summary table'
    [intro
      """At a glance, the headline trade-offs for each
         alternative. **Pick CX** is the column that matters
         most for an evaluator question."""]

    [table columns=(format, strength, weakness, pick-cx-when)
      [row format='JSON' strength='Universal ecosystem'
           weakness='No schema, no comments, no identity'
           pick-cx-when='You need any of: schema / comments / identity / mixed content / dates / bytes']
      [row format='YAML' strength='Human-readable config'
           weakness='Indentation footguns, lossy round-trip, Norway problem'
           pick-cx-when='You want YAML readability without the footguns']
      [row format='TOML' strength='Flat config readability'
           weakness='Awkward at depth, no schema'
           pick-cx-when='Your config is hierarchical or grows beyond config']
      [row format='XML' strength='Tree-shaped, mixed content, XPath ecosystem'
           weakness='Verbose, XSD complexity, no homoiconic code-as-data'
           pick-cx-when='You want the XML data model in a denser syntax with code-as-data unified']
      [row format='Protobuf' strength='Wire size and throughput'
           weakness='Schema-required, opaque without .proto, no document semantics'
           pick-cx-when='Your data is also config / docs / hashed payloads, not just RPC wire']
      [row format='MessagePack' strength='Compact binary JSON'
           weakness='Flat / no document semantics'
           pick-cx-when='Your data has shape (attributes / mixed content / namespaces)']
      [row format='Pkl' strength='Strongly typed config language'
           weakness='Config-only; no data identity, narrower output formats'
           pick-cx-when='The same documents are data and code, not just config']
      [row format='Dhall' strength='Total, sound, content-addressed imports'
           weakness='Steep learning curve, config-only'
           pick-cx-when='You want Dhall semantics plus rich data shape and broad outputs']
      [row format='Parquet' strength='Analytical scan, ecosystem'
           weakness='File-format only, no document semantics'
           pick-cx-when='Producer and consumer are CX-native; export to Parquet for interchange']
      [row format='Arrow' strength='Zero-copy in-memory columnar'
           weakness='No document semantics, no identity'
           pick-cx-when='You want Arrow plus CX semantics (use the bridge — they coexist)']]

    [body
      """**The honest summary.** CX wins on **unification**:
         data + code + schema + identity in one syntax. It
         loses on **ecosystem maturity** — JSON, YAML, and
         Protobuf all have decades of library / editor / CDN
         depth. The question for an evaluator is whether the
         unification value exceeds the ecosystem-depth gap for
         your specific workload. For workloads in §13.1's
         "otherwise" bucket, the answer in 2026 is increasingly
         yes; for the niches above the "otherwise" line, pick
         the specialist."""]

    [note
      """If you have a workload that does not match any row of
         this table or §13.1's decision tree, file an issue at
         https://github.com/cx-home/cx — the comparison is
         maintained as a living document; new alternatives and
         new use-case scenarios are added as the ecosystem
         evolves."""]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/comparison.cx during Phase B
     docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). At-a-glance feature
     table and conversion-loss matrix that complement the
     per-format children (§13.2-§13.11) above. #]

  [child id=cmp-at-a-glance n=13.13 title='At-a-glance feature matrix'
    [intro
      """One-screen feature matrix across CX, JSON, YAML, TOML,
         and XML. The per-format children (§13.2-§13.11) carry
         the depth; this matrix is the spreadsheet view."""]

    [body
      """| Feature | CX | JSON | YAML | TOML | XML |
         |---|---|---|---|---|---|
         | Syntax weight | brackets, no closing tags | curly braces + brackets | indent-significant | tables + key=val | open + close tags |
         | Strong types | ✅ int / float / bool / null / sized / decimal / bigint / date / datetime / bytes / atom | ❌ number only | partial (auto-detect) | ✅ int / float / bool / datetime | partial (xs:type) |
         | Comments | ✅ block and line | ❌ | ✅ # … | ✅ # … | ✅ |
         | Mixed content | ✅ first-class | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ first-class |
         | Multiple top-level docs | ✅ no wrapper required | ❌ requires array | ✅ via --- separator | ❌ single document | partial |
         | Attribute / element distinction | ✅ explicit | ❌ flat keys | ❌ flat keys | ❌ flat keys | ✅ explicit |
         | Type fidelity through round-trip | ✅ via CXCol | ❌ int↔float coerced | partial | ✅ preserved | partial |
         | Tabular data efficiency | ✅ `:table` block, columnar binary | ❌ verbose array-of-objects | ❌ verbose | partial (array of tables) | ❌ verbose |
         | Streaming parser | ✅ pull-based handle API | partial | ❌ usually whole-file | ❌ | ✅ SAX |
         | Built-in query language | ✅ CXPath value kind, all 12 XPath 3.1 axes | ❌ third-party (jq) | ❌ third-party (yq) | ❌ | ✅ XPath 1.0 / XQuery |
         | Pure-functional transform | ✅ `[?modify]` focus + 11 actions, structural sharing | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | partial (XSLT) |
         | Pattern-match dispatch | ✅ `[?match]` heterogeneous arms | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |
         | Module system | ✅ `[?def]` / `[?lib]` / cx.lock SRI | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | partial (XInclude) |"""]]

  [child id=cmp-loss-matrix n=13.14 title='Conversion-loss matrix'
    [intro
      """Lossless conversion means the data round-trips without
         semantic loss; presentation details (comments,
         whitespace, attribute order) may be normalized. The
         table below is the honest accounting of what survives,
         what gets normalized, and what is genuinely lossy."""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'CX → format → CX'] [th 'Lossless?'] [th 'What is preserved'] [th 'What is normalised or lost']]
      [tr [td 'CX → JSON → CX'] [td 'data ✅ / presentation 📋'] [td 'Element shape, attributes, types via CXCol, values, nesting'] [td 'Comments dropped; element vs attribute distinction collapses to keys']]
      [tr [td 'CX → YAML → CX'] [td 'data ✅ / presentation 📋'] [td 'Element shape, types via ' [code '!!tags'] ', multiline strings'] [td 'Comments dropped by most parsers; flow-vs-block style normalised']]
      [tr [td 'CX → TOML → CX'] [td 'data ✅ / presentation 📋'] [td 'Element shape via table headers, strong types'] [td 'Comments dropped; section ordering normalised; deep nesting flattened']]
      [tr [td 'CX → XML → CX'] [td 'data ✅ / presentation 📋'] [td 'Element shape, attributes, mixed content, namespaces, PIs'] [td 'Whitespace normalised per ' [code 'xml:space'] '; entity references resolved']]
      [tr [td 'CX → CXCol → CX'] [td 'bytes ✅'] [td 'Everything byte-for-byte'] [td 'Nothing — CXCol is the strict-canonical form']]
      [tr [td 'CX → CSV → CX'] [td 'data partial'] [td [code ':table'] ' block columns and rows'] [td 'Non-table data not encodable; cell subtyping collapses']]]

    [note
      [b 'Presentation-layer differences'] ' — comments (only XML '
      'and CX preserve them; JSON has none; YAML and TOML lose '
      'them through most parsers), whitespace (normalised to the '
      'target conventions), attribute order (emission may pick a '
      'canonical order), anchor names (canonical form renumbers '
      'them). These all show up as identical hashes via '
      [code 'cx hash'] ' because the hash operates on strict-canonical '
      'bytes, where presentation is stripped.']]

  [child id=cmp-when-cx n=13.15 title='When CX wins'
    [body
      [b 'CX is the right pick'] ' for typed configuration, mixed '
      'content alongside structured data, lossless conversion across '
      'formats, cases where you need a stable canonical form for '
      'hashing or signing, and any pipeline where the query/transform '
      'layer (' [code 'CXPath'] ' + ' [code '[?match]'] ' + '
      [code '[?modify]'] ') should live in the same syntax as the data.']]]
